191
00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:56.129
sankarshan: Original, driver for this came about because there was a recent, or not-so-recent incident, reported incident, where malicious actors

192
00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:07.779
sankarshan: became committers inside the Linux kernel workflow, and were attempting to inject flaws deliberately into a very, well-known utility, even though little,

193
00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:14.069
sankarshan: Care is given because it's one of those single maintainer kind of stuff, the XZ utils.

194
00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:30.789
sankarshan: And it reflected that the workflow that has been sustaining for so long based on PGP key exchange and vetting is not sufficiently robust in this world of malicious actors, AI agents, and all of that, so…

195
00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:46.530
sankarshan: The Decentralized trust graph and the verifiable trust community essentially is a way to ensure that there is better representation of the person in the digital space that is cryptographically backed up.

196
00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:51.679
sankarshan: And there is an audit trail that communities can use to be able to understand

197
00:22:51.840 --> 00:23:00.880
sankarshan: and have high confidence when you… when they're interacting over a transaction. So the idea is that everything digitally is a node.

198
00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.359
sankarshan: Transactions or interactions are edges.

199
00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:21.850
sankarshan: And that graph that gets created through this is decentralized and verifiable, and that allows you to have more robust insights into what exactly is happening. Obviously, this requires bootstrapping community, individuals having virtual agents and all of that.

200
00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:35.870
